## Navigating Local Lemon Law Claims

Lemon law claims in Orange County depend on meeting the qualifications of [California lemon law](/info/a-comprehensive-guide-to-california-lemon-law). Unfortunately, the first sign of a defect does not automatically mean you have a lemon. Manufacturers must be given a reasonable number of attempts to fix the defect.

Your vehicle must meet these qualifications before you’re eligible for legal recourse:

- The vehicle’s warranty was in effect when you first reported the defect to the manufacturer or dealership;

- The defect is “substantial” - meaning it compromises the vehicle’s functionality, safety, and/or value;

- The defect was not caused by driver error or neglect;

- The manufacturer’s certified repair facility has been given a reasonable number of attempts to correct the defect; or

- The vehicle has been in the certified repair facility (and out of service) for 30 or more total days.

Once you have official records and documentation to prove your vehicle checks these boxes, you may move forward with a lemon law claim to pursue a buyback or replacement vehicle.

